= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $t>0$, $B_t$ has the <normal distribution> $N(0,t)$. Evaluate its <Gaussian integral>:
$$
L(t,\alpha,0)
=\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi t}}\int_{\mathbb R}
\exp\!\left[-\left(\alpha+\frac1{2t}\right)x^2\right]dx
=\frac1{\sqrt{1+2\alpha t}}.
$$
At $t=0$ the defining random variable is one, consistent with the same formula. Thus
$$
\boxed{L(t,\alpha,0)=(1+2\alpha t)^{-1/2}\qquad(t,\alpha\geq0).}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $Y_t=-\beta\int_0^tB_u\,dB_u-\int_0^th_u\,du$. Its <quadratic variation> is $[Y]_t=\beta^2\int_0^tB_u^2du$. The <Itô formula> for $Z=e^Y$ gives
$$
dZ_t=-\beta Z_tB_t\,dB_t
+Z_t\left(\frac{\beta^2}{2}B_t^2-h_t\right)dt.
$$
Choose
$$
\boxed{h_t=\frac{\beta^2}{2}B_t^2,\qquad dZ_t=-\beta B_tZ_t\,dB_t,\quad Z_0=1.}
$$
The chosen process is continuous and <adapted>, hence <progressively measurable>. Its time integral is finite on compact intervals because Brownian paths are continuous. The resulting $Z$ is the <stochastic exponential> of $-\beta\int_0^\cdot B_u\,dB_u$, and is a positive <local martingale>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Itô formula> for $B_t^2$ yields $\int_0^tB_u\,dB_u=(B_t^2-t)/2$. Substituting into the expression for $Z_t$ gives the pathwise identity
$$
Z_t=\exp\!\left[-\frac\beta2B_t^2+\frac{\beta t}{2}
-\frac{\beta^2}{2}\int_0^tB_u^2\,du\right].
$$
Consequently
$$
Z_t\exp\!\left[\left(\frac\beta2-\alpha\right)B_t^2-\frac{\beta t}{2}\right]
=\exp\!\left[-\alpha B_t^2-\frac{\beta^2}{2}\int_0^tB_u^2\,du\right].
$$
Taking <expected values> proves
$$
\boxed{L(t,\alpha,\beta)
=\mathbb E\!\left[Z_t\exp\!\left(\left(\frac\beta2-\alpha\right)B_t^2-\frac{\beta t}{2}\right)\right].}
$$
The random variable on the preceding line is bounded by one for $\alpha\geq0$, so its integrability is immediate.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The expression obtained in part (a)(iii) gives a direct deterministic bound. For every fixed $T<\infty$ and $0\leq s\leq T$,
$$
0<Z_s
=\exp\!\left[\frac{\beta s}{2}-\frac\beta2B_s^2
-\frac{\beta^2}{2}\int_0^sB_u^2du\right]
\leq e^{\beta T/2}.
$$
Thus the <bounded local martingale criterion> makes $Z$ a true <martingale> on every finite time interval. More explicitly, if $\sigma_n$ is a <localizing sequence>, the identities $\mathbb E[Z_{t\wedge\sigma_n}\mid\mathcal F_s]=Z_{s\wedge\sigma_n}$ pass to the limit by <dominated convergence>, using the displayed bound for $t\leq T$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[Z_t\mid\mathcal F_s]=Z_s,\qquad \mathbb EZ_t=1\quad(0\leq s\leq t<\infty).}
$$
This establishes exactly the finite-horizon property needed for the subsequent change of measure.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The relation between $B$ and $W$ is the linear equation $dB_s=dW_s-\beta B_sds$ with $B_0=0$. Multiplication by the deterministic integrating factor $e^{\beta s}$ and the <Itô product rule> give
$$
d(e^{\beta s}B_s)=e^{\beta s}\,dW_s.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{B_s=\int_0^se^{-\beta(s-u)}\,dW_u
=W_s-\beta\int_0^se^{-\beta(s-u)}W_u\,du.}
$$
The second equality follows by <semimartingale integration by parts>. It expresses $B$ as an explicit function of the continuous path of $W$, using only an ordinary time integral. This inversion holds before changing measure; $W$ is already a <continuous semimartingale> under the original measure.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Fix $t<\infty$. Part (b)(i) makes $d\mathbb Q_t=Z_t\,d\mathbb P$ a <probability measure> on $\mathcal F_t$, equivalent to $\mathbb P$ since $Z_t>0$. Its density process on $[0,t]$ is $Z_s=\mathbb E[Z_t\mid\mathcal F_s]$. The <Girsanov theorem>, applied to the <stochastic exponential> with integrand $-\beta B_s$, says that
$$
W_s=B_s+\beta\int_0^sB_u\,du,\qquad 0\leq s\leq t,
$$
is standard <Brownian motion> under $\mathbb Q_t$. The required local square-integrability follows from path continuity, and the true <martingale> property was proved in part (b)(i).

By part (b)(ii), under $\mathbb Q_t$ the process $B$ is therefore the zero-started <Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process>:
$$
\boxed{dB_s=dW_s-\beta B_sds,\qquad
B_s=\int_0^se^{-\beta(s-u)}\,dW_u,\quad 0\leq s\leq t.}
$$
This identifies its full law on continuous path space, rather than just its one-time marginal. It is a centered <Gaussian process>, since it consists of deterministic <Itô integrals> against <Brownian motion>. For $r,s\in[0,t]$, the <Itô isometry> gives its <covariance function>:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cov}_{\mathbb Q_t}(B_r,B_s)
=\int_0^{r\wedge s}e^{-\beta(r-u)}e^{-\beta(s-u)}du
=\frac{e^{-\beta|s-r|}-e^{-\beta(s+r)}}{2\beta}.}
$$
In particular, its <Ornstein-Uhlenbeck transition distribution> at the terminal time is
$$
B_t\sim N(0,v_t),\qquad v_t=\frac{1-e^{-2\beta t}}{2\beta}.
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $c=\beta/2-\alpha$. The change of measure and part (a)(iii) yield
$$
L(t,\alpha,\beta)=e^{-\beta t/2}\mathbb E_{\mathbb Q_t}e^{cB_t^2}.
$$
For a centered <normal random variable> $G$ of <variance> $v>0$, a <Gaussian integral> gives the <Gaussian quadratic exponential moment>
$$
\mathbb Ee^{cG^2}=(1-2cv)^{-1/2},\qquad 1-2cv>0.
$$
Here $c\leq\beta/2$ and $\beta v_t<1/2$ for finite positive $t$, so the required positivity holds, including when $c>0$. Thus
$$
L(t,\alpha,\beta)
=\left[e^{\beta t}\left(1-(\beta-2\alpha)
\frac{1-e^{-2\beta t}}{2\beta}\right)\right]^{-1/2}.
$$
Expanding the two exponentials in terms of <hyperbolic functions> gives
$$
\boxed{L(t,\alpha,\beta)
=\left(\cosh(\beta t)+\frac{2\alpha}{\beta}\sinh(\beta t)\right)^{-1/2}\quad(\beta>0).}
$$
At $t=0$ this is one. As $\beta\downarrow0$, the denominator tends to $1+2\alpha t$, recovering part (a)(i). At $\alpha=0$ it gives the <Laplace transform of the integrated square of Brownian motion>. The joint terminal-and-integral transform is the <joint Laplace transform of Brownian square and its time integral>.

= 3
{parent=2025}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write the diffusion and <drift coefficients> as
$$
\sigma(x)=\frac1{1+x^2},\qquad b(x)=-\frac1{2(1+x^2)^2}.
$$
Both are continuously differentiable, with
$$
\sigma'(x)=-\frac{2x}{(1+x^2)^2},\qquad
b'(x)=\frac{2x}{(1+x^2)^3}.
$$
These derivatives are bounded. For example $2|x|\leq1+x^2$ bounds both absolute values by one. The <mean value theorem> therefore makes both coefficients <globally Lipschitz>. They are also bounded, so satisfy the <linear growth condition for an SDE>.

The <global existence theorem for stochastic differential equations with Lipschitz coefficients> consequently gives
$$
\boxed{\text{a global strong solution, unique up to indistinguishability}.}
$$
In particular, there is no finite explosion time. The solution has continuous paths, so all subsequent level-hitting and stopping arguments apply.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Apply the <Itô formula> to $M_t=e^{X_t}$. Since $d[X]_t=\sigma(X_t)^2dt$ and $b=-\sigma^2/2$, the two <drift> terms cancel:
$$
dM_t=e^{X_t}dX_t+\frac12e^{X_t}d[X]_t
=\frac{e^{X_t}}{1+X_t^2}\,dB_t.
$$
The integrand is continuous and hence locally square integrable after stopping at bounded levels of $X$. Thus $M$ is a positive <continuous local martingale>, with $M_0=1$ and <quadratic variation>
$$
\boxed{M_t=1+\int_0^t\frac{e^{X_s}}{1+X_s^2}\,dB_s,\qquad
[M]_t=\langle M\rangle_t=\int_0^t\frac{e^{2X_s}}{(1+X_s^2)^2}\,ds.}
$$
The cancellation is the reason $e^x$ is the relevant <scale function> for this diffusion.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take $L>0$, as needed for a nontrivial interval. Since $X$ is continuous and <adapted>, for every level $a$ and $t\geq0$,
$$
\{T_a\leq t\}
=\left\{\inf_{0\leq s\leq t}|X_s-a|=0\right\}.
$$
The infimum is attained on this compact interval and can be computed on the countable dense set $(\mathbb Q\cap[0,t])\cup\{t\}$. It is therefore $\mathcal F_t$-measurable. Thus $T_a$ is a <stopping time>, and so is $T=T_{-L}\wedge T_L$, because $\{T\leq t\}=\{T_{-L}\leq t\}\cup\{T_L\leq t\}$.

By continuity, $X_{t\wedge T}\in[-L,L]$, even before knowing that $T$ is finite. Hence
$$
e^{-L}\leq M_{t\wedge T}\leq e^L.
$$
Stopping preserves the <local martingale> property. The <bounded local martingale criterion> now shows that $M^T=(M_{t\wedge T})_{t\geq0}$ is a <uniformly integrable martingale>; its deterministic bound also gives square integrability. In particular,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb EM_{t\wedge T}=1,\qquad
\mathbb EM_{t\wedge T}^2\leq e^{2L}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The stopped representation from part (a)(ii) and the <Itô isometry> give the exact second-moment identity
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[M_{t\wedge T}^2]
=1+\mathbb E\int_0^{t\wedge T}\frac{e^{2X_s}}{(1+X_s^2)^2}\,ds.}
$$
The cross term with the initial value is zero because the stopped <stochastic integral> has zero mean. This is the useful finite-time computation; evaluating the remaining occupation integral explicitly is unnecessary for proving integrability of $T$.

For $|x|\leq L$,
$$
\frac{e^{2x}}{(1+x^2)^2}\geq c_L,
\qquad c_L=\frac{e^{-2L}}{(1+L^2)^2}>0.
$$
Combining the second-moment identity with part (i) yields
$$
1+c_L\mathbb E[t\wedge T]\leq\mathbb E[M_{t\wedge T}^2]\leq e^{2L}.
$$
Let $t\to\infty$ and use <monotone convergence>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb ET\leq e^{2L}(1+L^2)^2(e^{2L}-1)<\infty.}
$$
In particular $T<\infty$ <almost surely>, a conclusion obtained without assuming finite exit in the stopping argument.

One can also evaluate the limiting second moment exactly. At exit $M_T$ is either $e^{-L}$ or $e^L$, so
$$
M_T^2=(e^L+e^{-L})M_T-1.
$$
By <bounded convergence theorem> and part (i), $\mathbb EM_T=1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb E[M_{t\wedge T}^2]
=\mathbb EM_T^2=e^L+e^{-L}-1.}
$$
The same bound also holds at every finite time, by applying $(y-e^{-L})(y-e^L)\leq0$ to $y=M_{t\wedge T}$ and taking <expected values>. It improves the preceding bound to $\mathbb ET\leq c_L^{-1}(e^L+e^{-L}-2)$ if desired.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $S=T_b\wedge T_{-a}$ and $p=\mathbb P(T_b<T_{-a})$. Set $R=\max(a,b)$. The exit time $S$ from $(-a,b)$ is at most the exit time from $(-R,R)$, so part (ii) gives $\mathbb ES<\infty$. Continuity ensures that exactly one of the two different boundary levels is reached at $S$.

The stopped <local martingale> $e^{X_{t\wedge S}}$ lies in $[e^{-a},e^b]$, hence is a <uniformly integrable martingale>. The <optional sampling theorem>, justified at the terminal time also by <bounded convergence theorem>, gives
$$
1=\mathbb Ee^{X_S}=pe^b+(1-p)e^{-a}.
$$
Thus the <boundary hitting probability from a diffusion scale function> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(T_b<T_{-a})
=\frac{1-e^{-a}}{e^b-e^{-a}}
=\frac{e^a-1}{e^{a+b}-1}.}
$$
For integer $n\to\infty$, the events $\{T_b<T_{-n}\}$ increase to $\{T_b<\infty\}$. Indeed, on any path with finite $T_b$, continuity makes the path bounded below on $[0,T_b]$, so some lower barrier $-n$ has not yet been hit. The reverse inclusion is immediate. Taking the limit in the displayed probability gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(T_b<\infty)=e^{-b}\qquad(b>0).}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Since $X_0=0$, its <supremum> $X^\star$ is nonnegative. For $b>0$, continuity gives
$$
\{X^\star>b\}=\bigcup_{n\geq1}\{T_{b+1/n}<\infty\}.
$$
The events increase with $n$. Part (iii) therefore yields $\mathbb P(X^\star>b)=\lim_ne^{-(b+1/n)}=e^{-b}$. Letting $b\downarrow0$ gives $\mathbb P(X^\star>0)=1$; letting $b\to\infty$ gives finiteness <almost surely>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{X^\star\sim\operatorname{Exp}(1),\qquad
\mathbb P(X^\star\leq x)=
\begin{cases}0,&x<0,\\1-e^{-x},&x\geq0.\end{cases}}
$$
Its <probability density function> is $e^{-x}\mathbf1_{\{x>0\}}$, with no atom at zero. Using strict exceedances above also avoids presupposing that the infinite-horizon <supremum> is attained.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $K=\sup_x f(x)<\infty$ and let $C$ be a <Lipschitz constant> for $f$. Since
$$
|f(x)^2-f(y)^2|\leq2KC|x-y|,
$$
both coefficients $f$ and $-f^2/2$ are bounded and <globally Lipschitz>. The <global existence theorem for stochastic differential equations with Lipschitz coefficients> gives a unique global <strong solution of a stochastic differential equation>.

The same <Itô formula> cancellation as before gives
$$
d(e^{X_t})=e^{X_t}f(X_t)\,dB_t,\qquad
[e^X]_t=\int_0^te^{2X_s}f(X_s)^2\,ds.
$$
For $S=T_{-a}\wedge T_b$, the stopped exponential is bounded between $e^{-a}$ and $e^b$. Continuity and strict positivity of $f$ give
$$
c_{a,b}:=\min_{x\in[-a,b]}e^{2x}f(x)^2>0.
$$
The <Itô isometry> therefore yields
$$
1+c_{a,b}\mathbb E[t\wedge S]
\leq\mathbb E e^{2X_{t\wedge S}}\leq e^{2b}.
$$
The <monotone convergence theorem> proves $\mathbb ES<\infty$. The bounded <optional stopping> argument is now identical to part (b)(iii), and gives
$$
\mathbb P(T_b<T_{-a})=\frac{1-e^{-a}}{e^b-e^{-a}},\qquad
\mathbb P(T_b<\infty)=e^{-b}.
$$
Passing from level hitting to strict exceedances as in part (b)(iv),
$$
\boxed{X^\star\sim\operatorname{Exp}(1).}
$$
No global positive lower bound on $f$ was needed: compact-interval positivity suffices for each exit argument. The law is independent of the particular $f$ because the <scale function> is always $s(x)=e^x$: its <infinitesimal generator> satisfies
$$
\mathcal Ls=\frac{f^2}{2}(s''-s')=0.
$$
This is the <exponential scale function for a drift tied to diffusivity>. Changing $f$ changes how quickly the path is traversed, but leaves these boundary probabilities and the maximum law unchanged.
